.TH "RAIL0" "1" "Aug 2026" "rail0 CLI" ""

.SH NAME
rail0-webhooks-rotate-secret - Generate a new shared secret (returned once on the response)


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrail0 webhooks rotate-secret  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Issue a new shared secret for a webhook, returned once on the response.

.PP
The old secret stops verifying immediately — there is no overlap window. Any
delivery already in flight was signed with the old one, so a receiver that has
not yet been updated will reject it and the delivery counts as failed, which
feeds the circuit breaker. Update the receiver first where you can, or expect a
short window of failures.

.PP
Rotate on suspected exposure, and after anyone who had the secret loses
access.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for rotate-secret

.PP
\fB-y\fP, \fB--yes\fP[=false]
	Skip the confirmation prompt (required to run non-interactively)


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--base-url\fP=""
	RAIL0 gateway base URL (env: RAIL0_BASE_URL)

.PP
\fB--debug\fP[=false]
	Print HTTP request/response details to stderr

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	Force plain (uncolored) JSON output (useful in scripts)

.PP
\fB--pretty\fP[=false]
	Use the key-value/table view instead of JSON (terminal only)

.PP
\fB-q\fP, \fB--quiet\fP[=false]
	Print only the result's id/hash, one per line (useful in scripts)


.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
  rail0 webhooks rotate-secret whk_abc123
.EE


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBrail0-webhooks(1)\fP
